A||||1||Sillhouette.com||Terryk ECM question for the 3.4DOHC||||1||
Z||000000||Sillhouette.com||08-06-2000||12:09 PM||||Would you be able to make a 92 3.4 engine understand boost?.  I'm planning on a 6psi supercharger with the Grand National Map sensor.  All I need is someone that knows how to manipulate the fuel curve to accept this.  Oh yeah how much would it cost?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||08-06-2000||12:34 PM||||9396 ECM, yeah it can be done. Many tables have to be compressed to allow a 190 kPa of range and the map has to be scaled in. It's a fair amount of work to do it right. The 9396 doesn't support boost directly. Getting it to run and getting it to run well are two different things.<P>Frankly, I would try the 7730/7727 ECM and the 89/90 Grand Prix Turbo Code. It's SD (MAP), DIS, supports boost and I have it reasonably worked out. It doesn't support a manual transmission, but that could be dealt with. That was my plan for adding a turbo to the DOHC once I get the DANG THING IN THE CAR. Lol! No reason it wouldn't work with the blower. Are you going to use a bypass valve?<P>Either approach would take some tuning. There is no way to just go in, change a few things and have it work right out the gate. The GPT code would certainly be tons closer in the important areas. It would be mostly fueling and spark changes. There is no way the DOHC can run it's 26 degrees of advance under boost (the GPT runs 15 max). 12 at the most if you are lucky (that pesky compression ratio). Lots of table changes in the DOHC code. <P>Since the 9396 is very close to the 7727, you might even luck out and not have to change too many wires.<P>Cost? It's not so much cost as time. I have other projects going. What is your time-frame?<P>We can go off line with this.<P>Terry<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||5
